OpenSSL 4.1.0: ECH with a HelloRetryRequest fails when the client sends a custom extension of its own

Carried by Kuicksilver under ADR-0019 (docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md); pinned by its SHA-256 in gradle/openssl.toml,
applied by tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with the first OpenSSL release that fixes the
bug.

The bug: tls_construct_extensions (ssl/statem/extensions.c) resets the SENT and RECEIVED flags of the custom extensions
(custom_ext_init) at the start of a ClientHello only when ECH is not attempted. With ECH the second inner ClientHello after
a HelloRetryRequest adds the custom extensions again with their SENT flag still set from the first one, custom_ext_add
fails its assertion that the flag is clear, and the handshake ends with internal_error. QUIC always registers one
(quic_transport_parameters, through ossl_quic_tls_configure or a third-party stack's SSL_set_quic_tls_transport_params),
so every ECH handshake over QUIC that meets a HelloRetryRequest fails.

The fix: reset the flags at the start of every inner ClientHello (ch_depth 1), the second one included; the outer
ClientHello copies the extensions from the inner one and calls no add_cb, so its flags stay as the inner one left them.

--- a/ssl/statem/extensions.c
+++ b/ssl/statem/extensions.c
@@ -1187,7 +1187,14 @@
 
     /* Add custom extensions first */
 #ifndef OPENSSL_NO_ECH
-    if ((context & SSL_EXT_CLIENT_HELLO) != 0 && s->ext.ech.attempted == 0)
+    /*
+     * With ECH the inner ClientHello is built first (ch_depth == 1) and the
+     * outer one copies the custom extensions from it, so reset the flags at
+     * the start of each inner ClientHello, including the second one after a
+     * HelloRetryRequest.
+     */
+    if ((context & SSL_EXT_CLIENT_HELLO) != 0
+        && (s->ext.ech.attempted == 0 || s->ext.ech.ch_depth == 1))
         /* On the server side with initialise during ClientHello parsing */
         custom_ext_init(&s->cert->custext);
 #else
